1
00:00:00,147 --> 00:00:01,179
In Singapore,

2
00:00:01,199 --> 00:00:06,146
the boss of the most valuable company on Earth is having dinner with a Chinese spy.

3
00:00:06,166 --> 00:00:08,550
He's Jensen Huang, who runs Nvidia.

4
00:00:08,570 --> 00:00:13,116
She's Alice Huang, no relation, and she runs one of his biggest customers.

5
00:00:13,136 --> 00:00:14,888
But here's the strange part.

6
00:00:14,908 --> 00:00:18,292
Somewhere nearby, American agents are taking pictures.

7
00:00:18,312 --> 00:00:20,304
They know exactly who she is.

8
00:00:20,324 --> 00:00:21,974
Jensen has no idea.

9
00:00:21,994 --> 00:00:24,293
To our customers!

10
00:00:24,313 --> 00:00:25,295
To friends.

11
00:00:25,315 --> 00:00:29,731
Under that table, her company is buying billions of dollars of his chips.

12
00:00:29,751 --> 00:00:31,946
The kind China isn't allowed to have.

13
00:00:31,966 --> 00:00:37,162
This is the story of how China got a spy into that seat, and who was watching.

14
00:00:37,182 --> 00:00:43,838
It starts with a phone call from Washington.

15
00:00:43,858 --> 00:00:47,562
But to see why that call mattered, you need to know who picked it up.

16
00:00:47,582 --> 00:00:50,566
Because the man in the leather jacket didn't start at the top.

17
00:00:50,586 --> 00:00:53,041
He started in a bathroom in Kentucky.

18
00:00:53,061 --> 00:00:55,513
Jensen's story begins in Taiwan.

19
00:00:55,533 --> 00:01:00,077
At nine, he and his older brother were put on a plane to relatives in America.

20
00:01:00,097 --> 00:01:04,259
To pay for a good school, his parents sold almost everything they owned.

21
00:01:04,279 --> 00:01:06,151
Oneida Baptist Institute.

22
00:01:06,171 --> 00:01:08,275
A boarding school in Kentucky.

23
00:01:08,295 --> 00:01:09,429
Very strict.

24
00:01:09,449 --> 00:01:10,919
Strict is good.

25
00:01:10,939 --> 00:01:12,911
It wasn't a fancy boarding school.

26
00:01:12,931 --> 00:01:15,363
It was a reform school for troubled kids,

27
00:01:15,383 --> 00:01:17,655
and the relatives had signed them up by mistake.

28
00:01:17,675 --> 00:01:20,627
His brother was sent to work the school's tobacco fields.

29
00:01:20,647 --> 00:01:22,371
Jensen got the bathrooms.

30
00:01:22,391 --> 00:01:25,778
A hundred teenage boys' worth, every single day.

31
00:01:25,798 --> 00:01:28,290
I just wished they would be a bit more careful.

32
00:01:28,310 --> 00:01:32,239
His roommate was seventeen, covered in tattoos and knife scars,

33
00:01:32,259 --> 00:01:34,091
and taped up from his latest fight.

34
00:01:34,111 --> 00:01:36,183
He was the toughest kid in the school.

35
00:01:36,203 --> 00:01:37,397
And he couldn't read.

36
00:01:37,417 --> 00:01:38,492
What's this say?

37
00:01:38,512 --> 00:01:39,719
I can teach you.

38
00:01:39,739 --> 00:01:42,142
Then I teach you the bench press.

39
00:01:42,162 --> 00:01:42,462
Deal.

40
00:01:42,482 --> 00:01:47,221
Decades later, Jensen gave that school two million dollars for a new building.

41
00:01:47,241 --> 00:01:48,693
It's called Huang Hall.

42
00:01:48,713 --> 00:01:52,188
The boy who cleaned its bathrooms put his name on the front door.

43
00:01:52,208 --> 00:01:55,407
At fifteen, he took the graveyard shift at Denny's.

44
00:01:55,427 --> 00:01:56,546
Dishes first.

45
00:01:56,566 --> 00:01:57,283
And then?

46
00:01:57,303 --> 00:01:58,555
Then busboy.

47
00:01:58,575 --> 00:02:01,522
Then, if you're lucky, waiter.

48
00:02:01,542 --> 00:02:03,026
And then?

49
00:02:03,046 --> 00:02:04,584
Then more dishes.

50
00:02:04,604 --> 00:02:07,628
And in nineteen ninety-three, he went back to Denny's.

51
00:02:07,648 --> 00:02:08,540
Not to work.

52
00:02:08,560 --> 00:02:09,641
To start a company.

53
00:02:09,661 --> 00:02:14,125
In a corner booth in East San Jose, Jensen and two engineer friends,

54
00:02:14,145 --> 00:02:18,689
Chris Malachowsky and Curtis Priem, sketched a chip for three-D graphics.

55
00:02:18,709 --> 00:02:22,467
He liked the place because it was quieter than home, and the coffee was cheap.

56
00:02:22,487 --> 00:02:23,630
Anything else, boys?

57
00:02:23,650 --> 00:02:24,932
A graphics chip.

58
00:02:24,952 --> 00:02:26,598
We have pancakes.

59
00:02:26,618 --> 00:02:28,264
Then three more coffees.

60
00:02:28,284 --> 00:02:31,016
Each founder put in two hundred dollars.

61
00:02:31,036 --> 00:02:34,420
Six hundred dollars, about the price of a used couch,

62
00:02:34,440 --> 00:02:37,353
to take on the biggest chip companies in the world.

63
00:02:37,373 --> 00:02:38,822
It nearly died.

64
00:02:38,842 --> 00:02:42,537
When its make-or-break chip finally shipped in nineteen ninety-seven,

65
00:02:42,557 --> 00:02:45,489
Nvidia had about one month of payroll left.

66
00:02:45,509 --> 00:02:47,863
The company's unofficial motto became

67
00:02:47,883 --> 00:02:50,842
Our company is thirty days from going out of business.

68
00:02:50,862 --> 00:02:52,647
So how long have we got?

69
00:02:52,667 --> 00:02:54,184
Thirty days.

70
00:02:54,204 --> 00:02:56,515
And if the new chip doesn't sell?

71
00:02:56,535 --> 00:02:57,484
Twenty-nine.

72
00:02:57,504 --> 00:03:00,688
It sold, about a million of them in four months.

73
00:03:00,708 --> 00:03:03,420
And here's the trick that later made it a giant.

74
00:03:03,440 --> 00:03:07,904
A normal computer chip is one brilliant chef cooking a whole wedding dinner,

75
00:03:07,924 --> 00:03:09,056
one plate at a time.

76
00:03:09,076 --> 00:03:12,248
Nvidia's chip is ten thousand line cooks,

77
00:03:12,268 --> 00:03:15,351
each flipping one pancake at the very same moment.

78
00:03:15,371 --> 00:03:18,523
For video games, that meant explosions.

79
00:03:18,543 --> 00:03:23,687
Then researchers noticed that artificial intelligence needs exactly that: millions

80
00:03:23,707 --> 00:03:25,909
of tiny sums, all at once.

81
00:03:25,929 --> 00:03:29,029
ChatGPT was trained on a supercomputer built

82
00:03:29,049 --> 00:03:31,673
from tens of thousands of Nvidia chips.

83
00:03:31,693 --> 00:03:35,775
And suddenly every AI company on Earth was standing in the same line.

84
00:03:35,795 --> 00:03:37,431
One of everything, please.

85
00:03:37,451 --> 00:03:38,035
Take a number.

86
00:03:38,055 --> 00:03:41,037
Forty thousand?

87
00:03:41,057 --> 00:03:42,466
That's your place in line.

88
00:03:42,486 --> 00:03:43,498
By twenty twenty-five,

89
00:03:43,518 --> 00:03:48,638
about eighty-six of every hundred dollars spent on AI chips went to Nvidia.

90
00:03:48,658 --> 00:03:52,134
In twenty twenty-three, Denny's put a plaque on the booth.

91
00:03:52,154 --> 00:03:54,286
It calls it "the booth that launched a

92
00:03:54,306 --> 00:03:56,870
one-trillion-dollar company." At the party,

93
00:03:56,890 --> 00:04:01,094
the boss of that company put on an apron, balanced ten plates up his arm,

94
00:04:01,114 --> 00:04:02,170
and served the tables.

95
00:04:02,190 --> 00:04:03,384
Still remember how?

96
00:04:03,404 --> 00:04:04,434
Dishes first.

97
00:04:04,454 --> 00:04:10,202
Two years later, Nvidia became the first company ever worth five trillion dollars.

98
00:04:10,222 --> 00:04:14,365
Every dollar the three of them put into that booth had become about eight billion.

99
00:04:14,385 --> 00:04:18,697
The kid who scrubbed the toilets in Kentucky now holds the key to artificial

100
00:04:18,717 --> 00:04:19,656
intelligence.

101
00:04:19,676 --> 00:04:21,509
And when he talks about who should be allowed

102
00:04:21,529 --> 00:04:24,003
to buy his chips, Washington listens.

103
00:04:24,023 --> 00:04:27,156
Every AI lab on Earth needs this man.

104
00:04:27,176 --> 00:04:28,525
Then so do we.

105
00:04:28,545 --> 00:04:33,935
That made him the one man China needed most.

106
00:04:33,955 --> 00:04:35,699
October, twenty twenty-two.

107
00:04:35,719 --> 00:04:37,411
Washington calls Nvidia.

108
00:04:37,431 --> 00:04:40,722
Your best AI chips can't be sold to China anymore.

109
00:04:40,742 --> 00:04:43,150
And the slower ones we made just for China?

110
00:04:43,170 --> 00:04:44,232
Those too.

111
00:04:44,252 --> 00:04:45,004
China.

112
00:04:45,024 --> 00:04:47,696
Seventeen billion dollars a year.

113
00:04:47,716 --> 00:04:50,006
Thirteen percent of everything we sell.

114
00:04:50,026 --> 00:04:50,734
Was.

115
00:04:50,754 --> 00:04:52,254
How many chips do we need?

116
00:04:52,274 --> 00:04:53,846
For a real AI?

117
00:04:53,866 --> 00:04:55,248
Tens of thousands.

118
00:04:55,268 --> 00:04:56,490
And one costs?

119
00:04:56,510 --> 00:04:58,702
About thirty thousand dollars.

120
00:04:58,722 --> 00:05:00,499
A new car, per chip.

121
00:05:00,519 --> 00:05:02,090
And how many can we buy?

122
00:05:02,110 --> 00:05:02,750
Zero.

123
00:05:02,770 --> 00:05:04,316
By Jensen's own account,

124
00:05:04,336 --> 00:05:08,791
Nvidia's share of China's market went from ninety-five percent to zero.

125
00:05:08,811 --> 00:05:11,923
And he told Washington so, every chance he got.

126
00:05:11,943 --> 00:05:13,661
They're nanoseconds behind us.

127
00:05:13,681 --> 00:05:15,046
We've got to go compete.

128
00:05:15,066 --> 00:05:17,258
So China couldn't buy the chips.

129
00:05:17,278 --> 00:05:19,870
But it could get close to the man who makes them,

130
00:05:19,890 --> 00:05:23,275
and who argues with Washington about who gets to buy them.

131
00:05:23,295 --> 00:05:27,539
That's a job for China's Ministry of State Security, the MSS.

132
00:05:27,559 --> 00:05:31,111
Picture the FBI and the CIA in one building.

133
00:05:31,131 --> 00:05:32,675
It doesn't crack the safe.

134
00:05:32,695 --> 00:05:34,534
It befriends the man with the key.

135
00:05:34,554 --> 00:05:38,486
Nobody outside Beijing has said when Alice Huang was recruited.

136
00:05:38,506 --> 00:05:41,549
But an American memo later wrote down her job.

137
00:05:41,569 --> 00:05:43,321
This man makes the chips.

138
00:05:43,341 --> 00:05:45,220
And Washington listens to him.

139
00:05:45,240 --> 00:05:47,261
So what do I steal?

140
00:05:47,281 --> 00:05:48,085
Nothing.

141
00:05:48,105 --> 00:05:49,877
You learn what he knows.

142
00:05:49,897 --> 00:05:51,706
You help him see things our way.

143
00:05:51,726 --> 00:05:53,536
Why would he even talk to me?

144
00:05:53,556 --> 00:05:55,366
Because you'll be his customer.

145
00:05:55,386 --> 00:05:56,194
And then?

146
00:05:56,214 --> 00:05:57,424
We make friends.

147
00:05:57,444 --> 00:05:59,616
The key belonged to one man.

148
00:05:59,636 --> 00:06:03,831
And that man likes everybody.

149
00:06:03,851 --> 00:06:05,283
In twenty twenty-three,

150
00:06:05,303 --> 00:06:10,207
a Chinese gaming and cloud company called Seven Road split off its business outside

151
00:06:10,227 --> 00:06:13,715
China, moved it to Singapore, and renamed it Megaspeed.

152
00:06:13,735 --> 00:06:16,059
The managing director was Alice Huang.

153
00:06:16,079 --> 00:06:17,522
We make phone games.

154
00:06:17,542 --> 00:06:19,859
Now we make supercomputers.

155
00:06:19,879 --> 00:06:21,270
Since when?

156
00:06:21,290 --> 00:06:23,070
Since Singapore.

157
00:06:23,090 --> 00:06:24,202
Address?

158
00:06:24,222 --> 00:06:24,522
Singapore.

159
00:06:24,542 --> 00:06:26,583
America trusts Singapore.

160
00:06:26,603 --> 00:06:27,845
Business?

161
00:06:27,865 --> 00:06:28,746
Cloud computing.

162
00:06:28,766 --> 00:06:32,961
A cloud company needs mountains of chips, and nobody asks why.

163
00:06:32,981 --> 00:06:34,826
And the chips go where?

164
00:06:34,846 --> 00:06:37,766
Data centers in Malaysia and Indonesia.

165
00:06:37,786 --> 00:06:39,678
Not one chip goes to China.

166
00:06:39,698 --> 00:06:42,257
Then how does China use them?

167
00:06:42,277 --> 00:06:43,503
It rents them.

168
00:06:43,523 --> 00:06:46,607
Imagine a country that isn't allowed to buy cars.

169
00:06:46,627 --> 00:06:50,003
So you buy the cars, park them just over the border,

170
00:06:50,023 --> 00:06:51,885
and rent out the keys by the hour.

171
00:06:51,905 --> 00:06:56,741
Chinese companies, Alibaba among them, rented time on the chips the same way,

172
00:06:56,761 --> 00:06:58,798
and the chips never entered China.

173
00:06:58,818 --> 00:07:00,386
Can we buy your chips?

174
00:07:00,406 --> 00:07:01,426
No.

175
00:07:01,446 --> 00:07:02,816
But you can rent them.

176
00:07:02,836 --> 00:07:03,599
By the hour?

177
00:07:03,619 --> 00:07:04,862
By the warehouse.

178
00:07:04,882 --> 00:07:06,661
At the end of twenty twenty-three,

179
00:07:06,681 --> 00:07:10,933
everything Megaspeed owned came to about thirty-three million dollars.

180
00:07:10,953 --> 00:07:15,368
A nice little company, and not one the boss of Nvidia had any reason to meet.

181
00:07:15,388 --> 00:07:19,384
So she went and found him.

182
00:07:19,404 --> 00:07:21,536
Jensen Huang isn't hard to meet.

183
00:07:21,556 --> 00:07:25,936
The former Denny's dishwasher now runs a company worth about four and a half

184
00:07:25,956 --> 00:07:30,020
trillion dollars, and he still treats every customer like a regular.

185
00:07:30,040 --> 00:07:31,605
As his spokesman later put it:

186
00:07:31,625 --> 00:07:36,069
Meetings with customers are part of a CEO's job; Jensen talks with thousands of

187
00:07:36,089 --> 00:07:37,694
customer executives every year.

188
00:07:37,714 --> 00:07:40,713
She was about to become one of those thousands.

189
00:07:40,733 --> 00:07:43,237
June, twenty twenty-four, in Taipei.

190
00:07:43,257 --> 00:07:46,189
The big chip show, Computex, has just ended,

191
00:07:46,209 --> 00:07:49,461
and Jensen goes for whiskey with people from the AI world.

192
00:07:49,481 --> 00:07:50,613
Someone takes photos.

193
00:07:50,633 --> 00:07:53,500
In them, right beside him, is Alice Huang.

194
00:07:53,520 --> 00:07:54,785
Who haven't I met?

195
00:07:54,805 --> 00:07:55,517
Alice.

196
00:07:55,537 --> 00:07:56,589
Megaspeed.

197
00:07:56,609 --> 00:07:57,462
Singapore.

198
00:07:57,482 --> 00:07:58,454
Singapore!

199
00:07:58,474 --> 00:07:59,538
What are you building?

200
00:07:59,558 --> 00:08:00,902
A cloud company.

201
00:08:00,922 --> 00:08:02,566
Something big.

202
00:08:02,586 --> 00:08:04,146
Then welcome to the table!

203
00:08:04,166 --> 00:08:06,478
Nobody looks at those photos twice.

204
00:08:06,498 --> 00:08:07,929
Later, someone will.

205
00:08:07,949 --> 00:08:12,321
That same month, at Megaspeed's Malaysian arm, the paperwork starts.

206
00:08:12,341 --> 00:08:14,892
What do we put up as collateral?

207
00:08:14,912 --> 00:08:16,009
Everything.

208
00:08:16,029 --> 00:08:18,183
The whole Malaysian business?

209
00:08:18,203 --> 00:08:21,074
The whole business, every last desk.

210
00:08:21,094 --> 00:08:22,644
And the lender is?

211
00:08:22,664 --> 00:08:24,082
Owned by Alibaba.

212
00:08:24,102 --> 00:08:25,651
And the buying began.

213
00:08:25,671 --> 00:08:27,446
A Singapore company wants servers!

214
00:08:27,466 --> 00:08:28,123
How many?

215
00:08:28,143 --> 00:08:28,731
Yes.

216
00:08:28,751 --> 00:08:29,922
Shipment one hundred.

217
00:08:29,942 --> 00:08:31,575
Shipment one hundred and fifty.

218
00:08:31,595 --> 00:08:32,801
Shipment two hundred.

219
00:08:32,821 --> 00:08:33,780
What's in them?

220
00:08:33,800 --> 00:08:35,312
Nvidia's best.

221
00:08:35,332 --> 00:08:37,056
Nearly two billion dollars' worth.

222
00:08:37,076 --> 00:08:39,120
At thirty thousand dollars a chip?

223
00:08:39,140 --> 00:08:41,399
That's about sixty thousand chips.

224
00:08:41,419 --> 00:08:44,326
A parking lot of sixty thousand new cars.

225
00:08:44,346 --> 00:08:45,255
In one year.

226
00:08:45,275 --> 00:08:49,119
End of twenty twenty-three, we had thirty-three million dollars.

227
00:08:49,139 --> 00:08:49,798
Yes.

228
00:08:49,818 --> 00:08:53,040
End of twenty twenty-four, we have three billion.

229
00:08:53,060 --> 00:08:53,782
Yes.

230
00:08:53,802 --> 00:08:56,526
Two point nine billion of it is deposits.

231
00:08:56,546 --> 00:08:57,338
From who?

232
00:08:57,358 --> 00:08:58,374
Refundable.

233
00:08:58,394 --> 00:08:58,993
From...

234
00:08:59,013 --> 00:08:59,858
Refundable.

235
00:08:59,878 --> 00:09:02,890
About ninety times bigger in one year.

236
00:09:02,910 --> 00:09:07,634
If a lemonade stand went from thirty-three dollars to three thousand in a year,

237
00:09:07,654 --> 00:09:09,424
its parents would have questions.

238
00:09:09,444 --> 00:09:11,648
Then, late in twenty twenty-four,

239
00:09:11,668 --> 00:09:14,088
an official from America's Commerce Department

240
00:09:14,108 --> 00:09:15,928
walks into one of the Malaysian data

241
00:09:15,948 --> 00:09:16,540
centers.

242
00:09:16,560 --> 00:09:19,612
It's supposed to be full of humming AI servers.

243
00:09:19,632 --> 00:09:21,197
Where's the data center?

244
00:09:21,217 --> 00:09:23,186
You're standing in it.

245
00:09:23,206 --> 00:09:24,839
These are boxes.

246
00:09:24,859 --> 00:09:26,903
Very advanced boxes.

247
00:09:26,923 --> 00:09:28,890
I'm opening an investigation.

248
00:09:28,910 --> 00:09:30,926
The inspector wrote it all down.

249
00:09:30,946 --> 00:09:32,446
And nothing happened.

250
00:09:32,466 --> 00:09:34,910
Because on paper, nothing looked wrong.

251
00:09:34,930 --> 00:09:38,334
Every sale went to Singapore or Malaysia, not China.

252
00:09:38,354 --> 00:09:40,886
When Nvidia sent people to check her sites,

253
00:09:40,906 --> 00:09:43,218
every chip was exactly where it should be.

254
00:09:43,238 --> 00:09:47,806
Jensen later said they had "counted every one." That was the whole trick: the chips

255
00:09:47,826 --> 00:09:50,802
stayed put, and China rented them over the internet.

256
00:09:50,822 --> 00:09:55,841
What almost nobody knew was that American spies were already listening to her.

257
00:09:55,861 --> 00:09:59,153
When American spy agencies listen to a foreign target,

258
00:09:59,173 --> 00:10:03,397
and that target talks to an American, the reports hide the American's name.

259
00:10:03,417 --> 00:10:05,560
They just say "an American citizen."

260
00:10:05,580 --> 00:10:07,760
She's talking to an American citizen.

261
00:10:07,780 --> 00:10:09,470
Then request the name.

262
00:10:09,490 --> 00:10:11,039
It's Jensen Huang.

263
00:10:11,059 --> 00:10:12,633
Does he know who she is?

264
00:10:12,653 --> 00:10:13,385
Our read?

265
00:10:13,405 --> 00:10:13,943
No.

266
00:10:13,963 --> 00:10:15,908
How far back does it go?

267
00:10:15,928 --> 00:10:17,592
June, twenty twenty-four.

268
00:10:17,612 --> 00:10:19,430
A whiskey bar in Taipei.

269
00:10:19,450 --> 00:10:22,750
Then we watch.

270
00:10:22,770 --> 00:10:25,142
So where did all those servers come from?

271
00:10:25,162 --> 00:10:28,386
Mostly from Aivres, the American arm of Inspur,

272
00:10:28,406 --> 00:10:32,030
a Chinese server giant partly owned by the Chinese state,

273
00:10:32,050 --> 00:10:33,789
and on America's blacklist.

274
00:10:33,809 --> 00:10:34,521
Inspur?

275
00:10:34,541 --> 00:10:35,785
You're on the list.

276
00:10:35,805 --> 00:10:36,637
I'm Aivres.

277
00:10:36,657 --> 00:10:37,788
The American son.

278
00:10:37,808 --> 00:10:39,200
Aivres...

279
00:10:39,220 --> 00:10:40,303
Not on here.

280
00:10:40,323 --> 00:10:40,850
Thanks.

281
00:10:40,870 --> 00:10:45,856
December twenty twenty-four to January twenty twenty-six: four point six billion

282
00:10:45,876 --> 00:10:47,514
dollars of equipment in.

283
00:10:47,534 --> 00:10:49,798
And from Aivres?

284
00:10:49,818 --> 00:10:50,782
Four billion.

285
00:10:50,802 --> 00:10:54,856
Malaysia was the older cousin who buys beer for kids who are too young.

286
00:10:54,876 --> 00:10:57,500
Except this cousin never hands the beer over.

287
00:10:57,520 --> 00:10:59,356
He lets you drink it at his place.

288
00:10:59,376 --> 00:11:04,328
Trade records later counted at least four point eight billion dollars of AI gear

289
00:11:04,348 --> 00:11:08,212
with Nvidia inside, all going to her company's Malaysian sister firm.

290
00:11:08,232 --> 00:11:13,331
Nearly five billion dollars, to one company most people had never heard of.

291
00:11:13,351 --> 00:11:17,383
Real smugglers hid Nvidia chips next to live lobsters.

292
00:11:17,403 --> 00:11:22,435
America's big smuggling bust in December twenty twenty-five was worth a hundred and

293
00:11:22,455 --> 00:11:26,439
sixty million dollars, about three percent of Megaspeed's haul.

294
00:11:26,459 --> 00:11:28,451
Megaspeed didn't need lobsters.

295
00:11:28,471 --> 00:11:29,744
It had invoices.

296
00:11:29,764 --> 00:11:30,420
Cost?

297
00:11:30,440 --> 00:11:31,572
Some dinners.

298
00:11:31,592 --> 00:11:32,367
Some whiskey.

299
00:11:32,387 --> 00:11:33,132
Return?

300
00:11:33,152 --> 00:11:37,176
Four point eight billion dollars of AI gear our companies can use.

301
00:11:37,196 --> 00:11:40,315
And a seat next to the man who argues with Washington.

302
00:11:40,335 --> 00:11:43,265
Did he ever slip you anything under the table?

303
00:11:43,285 --> 00:11:44,657
Not once.

304
00:11:44,677 --> 00:11:48,484
Why pass chips under the table when you can rent the whole warehouse?

305
00:11:48,504 --> 00:11:51,945
We make friends.

306
00:11:51,965 --> 00:11:54,377
So what was all of this really for?

307
00:11:54,397 --> 00:11:56,509
The seat mattered more than the chips.

308
00:11:56,529 --> 00:12:01,693
Not for blueprints: Nvidia says customers get "zero information relevant to national

309
00:12:01,713 --> 00:12:04,197
security." The prize was the man,

310
00:12:04,217 --> 00:12:07,798
because Washington couldn't make up its mind about what China could buy.

311
00:12:07,818 --> 00:12:08,951
Your China chip?

312
00:12:08,971 --> 00:12:10,236
Blocked.

313
00:12:10,256 --> 00:12:12,206
So we can't sell it?

314
00:12:12,226 --> 00:12:12,851
You can.

315
00:12:12,871 --> 00:12:14,264
We take fifteen percent.

316
00:12:14,284 --> 00:12:16,333
So we can sell it?

317
00:12:16,353 --> 00:12:17,772
And the stronger one too.

318
00:12:17,792 --> 00:12:20,047
We take twenty-five percent.

319
00:12:20,067 --> 00:12:22,253
I'm going to need a bigger whiteboard.

320
00:12:22,273 --> 00:12:23,145
Blocked.

321
00:12:23,165 --> 00:12:24,677
Then fifteen percent.

322
00:12:24,697 --> 00:12:26,206
Then twenty-five.

323
00:12:26,226 --> 00:12:29,274
Washington changes its mind every few months.

324
00:12:29,294 --> 00:12:31,646
And who does Washington keep hearing from?

325
00:12:31,666 --> 00:12:32,200
Him.

326
00:12:32,220 --> 00:12:36,724
That first call alone cost Nvidia four and a half billion dollars,

327
00:12:36,744 --> 00:12:38,696
in chips it suddenly couldn't sell.

328
00:12:38,716 --> 00:12:43,652
That's about thirteen dollars from every person in America, gone in one phone call.

329
00:12:43,672 --> 00:12:45,656
And through every one of those calls,

330
00:12:45,676 --> 00:12:49,700
Jensen made the same argument he'd been making for years, out loud,

331
00:12:49,720 --> 00:12:54,115
on every stage he could find: let American chips compete, everywhere.

332
00:12:54,135 --> 00:12:56,117
So you want to sell to China?

333
00:12:56,137 --> 00:12:59,414
I want the whole world building on American chips.

334
00:12:59,434 --> 00:13:00,468
Hold that thought.

335
00:13:00,488 --> 00:13:04,199
In Taipei, in May twenty twenty-five, he put it bluntly.

336
00:13:04,219 --> 00:13:06,880
All in all, the export control was a failure.

337
00:13:06,900 --> 00:13:08,871
A week later, to his investors:

338
00:13:08,891 --> 00:13:12,791
The fifty billion dollar China market is effectively closed to U.S.

339
00:13:12,811 --> 00:13:13,327
industry.

340
00:13:13,347 --> 00:13:15,231
Fifty billion dollars.

341
00:13:15,251 --> 00:13:19,387
That's about a hundred and fifty dollars for every American, left on the table.

342
00:13:19,407 --> 00:13:23,851
That autumn in London, he warned that China was going to win the AI race,

343
00:13:23,871 --> 00:13:26,183
with cheaper power and fewer rules.

344
00:13:26,203 --> 00:13:29,622
That same night, he made sure nobody missed whose side he was on.

345
00:13:29,642 --> 00:13:34,354
It's vital that America wins by racing ahead and winning developers worldwide.

346
00:13:34,374 --> 00:13:36,378
That's Jensen in one line.

347
00:13:36,398 --> 00:13:37,950
He wants America to win,

348
00:13:37,970 --> 00:13:41,355
and he wants to sell the whole world the tools to build on it.

349
00:13:41,375 --> 00:13:43,027
Now look at it from Beijing.

350
00:13:43,047 --> 00:13:46,091
One chip is worth about thirty thousand dollars.

351
00:13:46,111 --> 00:13:49,703
One conversation between this man and the White House can open a

352
00:13:49,723 --> 00:13:52,539
fifty-billion-dollar market, or close it.

353
00:13:52,559 --> 00:13:55,171
Which one would you rather have a seat next to?

354
00:13:55,191 --> 00:13:56,819
What is he telling Washington?

355
00:13:56,839 --> 00:13:58,791
Let American chips compete.

356
00:13:58,811 --> 00:13:59,523
Everywhere.

357
00:13:59,543 --> 00:14:02,535
He says that on every stage in the world.

358
00:14:02,555 --> 00:14:03,942
Why do we need you?

359
00:14:03,962 --> 00:14:06,034
Because I hear it first.

360
00:14:06,054 --> 00:14:07,722
And I see who he says it to.

361
00:14:07,742 --> 00:14:11,286
That's the job the memo would later describe: learn what he knows,

362
00:14:11,306 --> 00:14:13,981
and sit beside the man Washington listens to.

363
00:14:14,001 --> 00:14:15,805
And Washington kept moving.

364
00:14:15,825 --> 00:14:17,617
In December twenty twenty-five,

365
00:14:17,637 --> 00:14:21,069
Washington let approved Chinese buyers have the stronger chip.

366
00:14:21,089 --> 00:14:24,805
That same week, prosecutors announced that big smuggling bust,

367
00:14:24,825 --> 00:14:26,880
the one Megaspeed made look small.

368
00:14:26,900 --> 00:14:29,468
You're under arrest for sending these to China.

369
00:14:29,488 --> 00:14:30,412
Approved.

370
00:14:30,432 --> 00:14:32,005
What did you just approve?

371
00:14:32,025 --> 00:14:33,394
Sending these to China.

372
00:14:33,414 --> 00:14:36,586
Even the approved buyers weren't always what they seemed.

373
00:14:36,606 --> 00:14:38,472
New Matrix, Beijing.

374
00:14:38,492 --> 00:14:38,862
Approved.

375
00:14:38,882 --> 00:14:39,516
Hello?

376
00:14:39,536 --> 00:14:40,066
Anyone?

377
00:14:40,086 --> 00:14:42,578
The New York Times found that New Matrix,

378
00:14:42,598 --> 00:14:46,590
a seventeen-month-old firm Washington had cleared to handle those chips,

379
00:14:46,610 --> 00:14:49,402
had an office that was "a hollow gray shell

380
00:14:49,422 --> 00:14:51,554
with wires dangling from the ceiling."

381
00:14:51,574 --> 00:14:53,406
On paper, it was approved.

382
00:14:53,426 --> 00:14:55,901
On paper, Megaspeed looked fine too.

383
00:14:55,921 --> 00:14:59,845
Back in the middle of twenty twenty-five, Megaspeed had gone quiet.

384
00:14:59,865 --> 00:15:04,557
It stopped buying and walked away from an order worth three point two billion

385
00:15:04,577 --> 00:15:05,240
dollars.

386
00:15:05,260 --> 00:15:07,192
In October twenty twenty-five,

387
00:15:07,212 --> 00:15:11,104
Senator Tom Cotton asked Commerce to investigate Megaspeed.

388
00:15:11,124 --> 00:15:13,058
He called the company it came from

389
00:15:13,078 --> 00:15:15,753
A Chinese government-owned cloud company.

390
00:15:15,773 --> 00:15:19,365
The New York Times found the two billion dollars of chips.

391
00:15:19,385 --> 00:15:22,994
Nvidia said Megaspeed had "no China shareholders."

392
00:15:23,014 --> 00:15:25,906
In February twenty twenty-six, her data-center

393
00:15:25,926 --> 00:15:28,230
landlord, a business of Bain Capital,

394
00:15:28,250 --> 00:15:32,086
gave all the power it had set aside for Megaspeed to someone else.

395
00:15:32,106 --> 00:15:34,846
And the Commerce case moved to the Justice Department.

396
00:15:34,866 --> 00:15:41,533
By then, the people listening to her phone had a lot more than boxes.

397
00:15:41,553 --> 00:15:43,545
They had watched her for about a year.

398
00:15:43,565 --> 00:15:45,737
They photographed the dinners in Singapore.

399
00:15:45,757 --> 00:15:47,389
In May twenty twenty-five,

400
00:15:47,409 --> 00:15:49,749
there's even video of the two of them leaving

401
00:15:49,769 --> 00:15:52,221
a dinner with AI suppliers in Taipei,

402
00:15:52,241 --> 00:15:54,034
Alice a few feet behind him.

403
00:15:54,054 --> 00:15:57,598
In the spring of twenty twenty-six, America wrote it down,

404
00:15:57,618 --> 00:16:01,190
in a memo overseen by Secretary of State Marco Rubio.

405
00:16:01,210 --> 00:16:04,002
Recruited by China's Ministry of State Security.

406
00:16:04,022 --> 00:16:06,174
To gather information on Jensen Huang.

407
00:16:06,194 --> 00:16:08,989
And to shape his lobbying for chip sales to China.

408
00:16:09,009 --> 00:16:10,831
Who else sees this?

409
00:16:10,851 --> 00:16:11,790
Just this table.

410
00:16:11,810 --> 00:16:14,270
The memo went round that small group before

411
00:16:14,290 --> 00:16:16,270
President Trump's trip to Beijing in

412
00:16:16,290 --> 00:16:16,662
May.

413
00:16:16,682 --> 00:16:18,434
According to The New York Times, the

414
00:16:18,454 --> 00:16:21,002
President had been briefed, and didn't act.

415
00:16:21,022 --> 00:16:23,694
Jensen had been left off the list for that plane.

416
00:16:23,714 --> 00:16:24,986
Then Trump called him,

417
00:16:25,006 --> 00:16:31,228
and Jensen flew to Alaska to climb aboard Air Force One on the way.

418
00:16:31,248 --> 00:16:34,120
On the twenty-ninth of October, twenty twenty-six,

419
00:16:34,140 --> 00:16:38,312
The New York Times broke the story: Alice Huang had been passing information to

420
00:16:38,332 --> 00:16:40,996
Beijing, and American intelligence knew it.

421
00:16:41,016 --> 00:16:43,528
Nvidia's stock fell about three percent.

422
00:16:43,548 --> 00:16:47,098
Jensen told Bloomberg her contacts were about Megaspeed.

423
00:16:47,118 --> 00:16:49,798
It was all completely normal and not unusual.

424
00:16:49,818 --> 00:16:51,750
He said she no longer worked there.

425
00:16:51,770 --> 00:16:53,248
Beijing answered too.

426
00:16:53,268 --> 00:16:56,006
Nothing more than an attempt to smear China.

427
00:16:56,026 --> 00:17:00,486
Three congressmen asked Jensen in for a closed-door briefing on the eighteenth of

428
00:17:00,506 --> 00:17:01,178
November.

429
00:17:01,198 --> 00:17:02,619
Their letter warned of

430
00:17:02,639 --> 00:17:05,421
Significant national security concerns.

431
00:17:05,441 --> 00:17:08,656
On the sixth of November, the memo itself leaked.

432
00:17:08,676 --> 00:17:09,854
The spy memo's out!

433
00:17:09,874 --> 00:17:10,612
I know.

434
00:17:10,632 --> 00:17:12,011
How bad is it?

435
00:17:12,031 --> 00:17:14,720
We're up two point seven eight percent.

436
00:17:14,740 --> 00:17:15,205
Up?

437
00:17:15,225 --> 00:17:17,579
Today's news will not slow NVIDIA.

438
00:17:17,599 --> 00:17:20,351
But here's the part that still has no answer.

439
00:17:20,371 --> 00:17:22,343
Why did nobody tell Jensen?

440
00:17:22,363 --> 00:17:26,547
When the government finds a spy close to an American, there's a way to tell them.

441
00:17:26,567 --> 00:17:28,599
It's called a defensive briefing.

442
00:17:28,619 --> 00:17:33,005
In twenty fifteen, the FBI gave one to a congressman in California.

443
00:17:33,025 --> 00:17:35,985
Your fundraiser works for Chinese intelligence.

444
00:17:36,005 --> 00:17:37,208
Then she's gone.

445
00:17:37,228 --> 00:17:38,320
He cut her off.

446
00:17:38,340 --> 00:17:40,432
Jensen Huang never got that visit.

447
00:17:40,452 --> 00:17:44,156
The people who knew unmasked his name, photographed his dinners,

448
00:17:44,176 --> 00:17:46,491
wrote the memo and briefed the President.

449
00:17:46,511 --> 00:17:48,589
Nobody told the man at the table.

450
00:17:48,609 --> 00:17:50,193
He had no idea.

451
00:17:50,213 --> 00:17:53,685
And the memo says the friendship became more than a friendship.

452
00:17:53,705 --> 00:17:57,533
He was the target, and she got closer than anyone was ever meant to.

453
00:17:57,553 --> 00:18:00,541
On the eighth of November, Jensen wrote to his staff.

454
00:18:00,561 --> 00:18:03,505
I owe my family and all of you an apology.

455
00:18:03,525 --> 00:18:05,137
I was unfaithful to my wife.

456
00:18:05,157 --> 00:18:07,349
My actions hurt her and our children.

457
00:18:07,369 --> 00:18:09,281
There is no excuse for what I did.

458
00:18:09,301 --> 00:18:10,466
I am deeply sorry.

459
00:18:10,486 --> 00:18:12,612
Nvidia's board set up a committee of

460
00:18:12,632 --> 00:18:15,344
independent directors with outside lawyers.

461
00:18:15,364 --> 00:18:17,251
So far, it sees

462
00:18:17,271 --> 00:18:21,138
No reason to believe that either national security or NVIDIA secrets were

463
00:18:21,158 --> 00:18:21,914
compromised.

464
00:18:21,934 --> 00:18:23,886
Jensen is still CEO.

465
00:18:23,906 --> 00:18:26,558
And here's where his story reaches your wallet.

466
00:18:26,578 --> 00:18:31,742
Nvidia is now about eight dollars of every hundred in an S&P five hundred index

467
00:18:31,762 --> 00:18:32,154
fund.

468
00:18:32,174 --> 00:18:35,777
If you have a retirement account, there's a good chance you own a slice of him.

469
00:18:35,797 --> 00:18:36,369
Wait.

470
00:18:36,389 --> 00:18:37,620
I own Nvidia?

471
00:18:37,640 --> 00:18:39,167
Everybody owns Nvidia.

472
00:18:39,187 --> 00:18:42,759
And one scary headline from China can move it hard.

473
00:18:42,779 --> 00:18:45,871
In January twenty twenty-five, a Chinese AI

474
00:18:45,891 --> 00:18:48,590
lab called DeepSeek shocked Wall Street.

475
00:18:48,610 --> 00:18:50,779
A Chinese lab built a cheap AI!

476
00:18:50,799 --> 00:18:54,616
We just lost five hundred and eighty-nine billion dollars.

477
00:18:54,636 --> 00:18:55,944
How much is that?

478
00:18:55,964 --> 00:18:59,526
About seventeen hundred dollars for every person in America.

479
00:18:59,546 --> 00:19:03,034
It was the biggest one-day loss in market history.

480
00:19:03,054 --> 00:19:06,406
Nvidia reports its earnings on the seventeenth of November.

481
00:19:06,426 --> 00:19:08,778
The congressmen get their briefing the next day.

482
00:19:08,798 --> 00:19:12,482
And two weeks later, Jensen walks onto a stage in Washington,

483
00:19:12,502 --> 00:19:15,276
the same city as the people who never told him.

484
00:19:15,296 --> 00:19:16,388
And Alice Huang?

485
00:19:16,408 --> 00:19:17,880
She's gone from Megaspeed.

486
00:19:17,900 --> 00:19:20,712
Her name has been taken off its company papers.

487
00:19:20,732 --> 00:19:22,493
Nobody has said where she is.

488
00:19:22,513 --> 00:19:24,425
Go back to that dinner in Singapore.

489
00:19:24,445 --> 00:19:26,729
The candles, the toast to friends.

490
00:19:26,749 --> 00:19:29,172
The chair next to Jensen is empty now.

491
00:19:29,192 --> 00:19:30,200
We make friends.

492
00:19:30,220 --> 00:19:35,164
America watched her at his table for almost two years, and never told him.

493
00:19:35,184 --> 00:19:39,808
So who's pulling up a chair next to the people who build America's AI tonight?

494
00:19:39,828 --> 00:19:42,044
Jensen ended his letter like this.

495
00:19:42,064 --> 00:19:44,356
I love our country and our company.

496
00:19:44,376 --> 00:19:47,686
I am proud and grateful to be an American.

